  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13\n\nI ran `word_count(\"\")` first and it does return `1`. The cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`: the function uses `text.split(\" \")`, and splitting an empty string that way gives `['']`, a list with one item, so the count is 1.\n\nThe issue also notes that splitting on a single space miscounts other input:\n- **Double spaces:** `\"a  b\"` returns 3.\n- **Tabs or newlines:** `\"a\\tb\"` returns 1.\n- **Leading or trailing spaces:** `\" a \"` returns 3.\n\nIt suggests switching to `text.split()`, which handles all of these cases and is what `average_word_length` already uses. It also includes a test for the empty string. I didn't change any code.",
